  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM EDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nToday-Tonight... The upper level cut-off low and associated \nnor'easter will move northward along the NE coastline through today. \nAt the surface, broad high pressure will continue to build over the \nFlorida peninsula as high pressure across the western Atlantic \nslowly builds westward towards the Caribbean. Locally, drier air \nwill filter across the area, with forecast PW values falling to 1.0-\n1.2\" areawide. This will result in little to no rain and storm \nchances today, with the highest rain chances (PoP 20 percent) \noccurring across the southern Treasure Coast. The pressure gradient \nremains slack over the local area, with light and variable winds \ntoday turning onshore (east to southeast) in the afternoon with the \nformation of the east coast sea breeze. Temperatures will be \nseasonable today, however they will begin to increase from the past \ncouple days. Afternoon highs will be in the mid to upper 80s, and \nmaybe even reach 90 degrees in some spots in the interior. Peak heat \nindices will be in the upper 80s to low 90s. Overnight lows will be \nin the 60s, possibly reaching 70 degrees along the coast. \n\nAt the beaches, hazards conditions linger. A High risk of dangerous \nlife-threatening rip currents continues at all area beaches. \nEntering the surf is strongly discouraged!\n\nTuesday-Wednesday... Upper level trough over the eastern seaboard \nearly next week will push offshore by midweek. A surface high \npressure in the western Atlantic will slowly shift northward through \nthe period, with the axis continuing to build over the Florida \npeninsula. Locally, onshore (east to southeast) winds will remain in \nplace, with speeds generally 5-10 mph. The east coast sea breeze \nwill form each afternoon, enhancing the winds to around 10 mph \nbehind the sea breeze. Moisture will begin to increase through \nperiod, with forecast PW values of 1.3-1.6\" on Tuesday afternoon \nbecoming to 1.6-1.8\" Wednesday afternoon. This will support \nincreasing rain and storm chances each day, becoming above normal by \nmid week. Highest rain chances will occur across the south, \ngenerally south of Orlando metro, each day. \n\nTemperatures will begin a warming trend, with seasonable to slightly \nabove normal conditions forecast. Afternoon highs will generally \nreach mid to upper 80s, with some locations reaching low 90s across \nthe interior. Overnight lows will be in the upper 60s to low 70s.  \n\nThursday-Sunday... Upper cut-off low over the Great Plains on \nThursday will shift northward, becoming absorbed into the trough \nover the Midwest by the weekend. An upper high pressure over the \nwestern Atlantic will shift westward, towards the Florida peninsula, \nthrough late week, with the center moving over Florida by the \nweekend. At the surface, high pressure over the western Atlantic \nwill steadily shift north and westward, moving towards the Carolina \ncoast. Locally, east to southeast winds will dominate, becoming \nenhanced each afternoon with the formation of the east coast sea \nbreeze. Moisture increases over the area through the period, with \nforecast PW values ranging from 2.0-2.4\". This will support near to \nabove normal rain chances each day, with scattered to numerous \nshowers and storms areawide by the weekend. Slight warming trend \ncontinues through the period, with seasonable to slightly above \nnormal temperatures forecast each day, as afternoon highs increasing \nabout a degree in some locations each day. Overnight lows will be in \nthe low to mid 70s.",
